    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Gunnar Henderson** and **Kyle Tucker** serving as our primary offensive engines. **Gunnar Henderson** (81.88) benefits from a prime leadoff spot and a significant SP skill advantage against Littell, with the analytical model aggressively weighting his elite speed potential despite the catcher's arm threat. Meanwhile, **Kyle Tucker** (81.26) anchors our outfield against Kochanowicz; with the opposing starter\u2019s high xERA, **Kyle Tucker** is positioned to provide elite power and counting stats. **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (72.73) and **Jackson Merrill** (68.69) also represent high-floor plays, with both benefiting from favorable platoon alignments and leadoff or near-leadoff placement, ensuring maximum plate appearance volume against mid-tier starting pitching.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved the marginal range, where **Coby Mayo** (49.85) just missed the cut. While **Coby Mayo** carries a respectable projection, his placement in the 8th spot of the order and the presence of a tough holding pitcher effectively neutralized his upside compared to the efficiency provided by **Brady House** (56.25). **Brady House** gets the nod primarily due to the specific conditions of his matchup against Baz, where a slow delivery increases his stolen base equity\u2014a critical tie-breaker in our 5x5 strategy. We continue to monitor the status of **Liam Hicks**, whose dismal 32.77 score against a tough left-handed arm keeps him firmly on the bench, well below our efficiency floor.\n\nManagement must remain wary of the environment surrounding **Drake Baldwin** (46.72). While he is our starting catcher, the matchup against Early presents a significant challenge, with a low xERA and neutral platoon splits suppressing his raw output. Similarly, while **Andy Pages** (66.83) and **Heliot Ramos** (62.63) are strong starts, both are impacted by minor \"Elite Catcher\" or \"Elite Hold\" penalties in our projection system, which accounts for the suppressed probability of successful stolen bases today. We are playing for consistent production across the board, trusting the optimizer\u2019s preference for leadoff volume over the occasional power-heavy, low-OBP profile of players like **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (59.49) or **Josh Jung** (59.79), both of whom are projected to be productive but lack the elite speed components required to carry the team\u2019s total Roto score.",
